<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="instrument_stereo_render">

  <!-- PROPOSAL 37, STEREO GATES (2026-08-17). THE GENERATOR MAPPING ROWS, READ
       OUT OF A RENDERED FILE, PER CHANNEL.

       Proposal 37's instrument phases (P3a/P3b/P3c) were designed and built
       while the sink was still mono, so every audio assertion in the
       instrument_* cases reads CHANNEL 0 and each of them carries the line
       "STEREO IS NOT GATED (proposal 36 B5)". Since 36-B5 the sink is as wide
       as the project (`RenderSession` writes the project's channel count), and
       `L != R` on a FILE is a legitimate claim. This case is where the four
       generator rows of `twPluginSlotProcessor` (plugins/CONTRACT.md inv. 16)
       stop being a statement about the engine and become a statement about the
       bytes in a WAV.

       ================================================== which row is which

       The row is chosen from the INSTANCE's ioLayout() against the PAGE WIDTH,
       i.e. the project's `channels=` (twpluginslotproc.cc, "THE GENERATOR
       ROWS"). `tw.test.clap.sine` reports 2 outputs — its MAIN bus; the mono
       AUX bus is not part of ioLayout() — and `tw.native.303` reports 1. So:

         sine on channels=2   0 -> C           DirectGen    (part A)
         sine on channels=1   0 -> 2, C == 1   GenFold      (part B)
         sine on channels=6   0 -> 2, 1<2<6    REFUSED      (part C)
         303  on channels=2   0 -> 1, C > 1    MonoSpread   (part D)

       PART C IS NOT A TYPO AND WIDEGEN IS NOT REACHABLE HERE. WideGen is
       `0 -> M with M > C`, and the GenFold row (`0 -> 2 on C == 1`) is tested
       first, so a 2-out instrument can never take it: reaching WideGen needs an
       instrument with at least THREE main outputs and no in-repo fixture has
       one. It stays gated at engine level only, in
       `test_plugin_insert.cc` ("0-in/4-out on 2 channels is WideGen"). What a
       2-out instrument on a SIX-channel page actually gets is the last row —
       "a generator narrower than the page but not mono: no defined spread, so
       refuse rather than guess" — which is `Transparent` + `Unsupported`, and
       which nothing else in the suite asserts.

       ============================================ the fixture and its param

       `tw.test.clap.sine` used to write the SAME sample to every main channel,
       which cannot distinguish a wide sink from one duplicating channel 0. It
       now carries **param id 3, "Stereo Skew", stepped, DEFAULT 0 = OFF**: off,
       every main channel is the same sample (so every render made before
       2026-08-17 is byte-identical and the existing channel-0 closed forms are
       untouched); on, channels 1.. of the main bus are at HALF amplitude.

       THE CLOSED FORM. 16 envelope-less sine voices, amp = velocity, instant
       on/off, and the ABI carries velocity NORMALIZED, so `velocity="100"` is
       100/127 at the plugin. A held note's rms over a whole second is

           ch0 = (100/127)/sqrt(2) * gain = 0.556769
           ch1 = ch0 / 2                  = 0.278384      (skew ON)
           ch1 = ch0                                      (skew OFF)

       +/-3 % bands throughout: that is the 16-bit round trip plus the two
       4096-frame chunk boundaries per second (4096 does not divide 48000), and
       nothing else — the sine has no envelope to smear it.

       A TRAP WORTH THE LINE IT COSTS: `set-project-channels` RE-DERIVES the
       mapping, and re-deriving RE-INSTANTIATES the plugin
       (`SPluginSlot::ensureChannels`, "the bypass flag and the saved state
       chunk have to be re-applied every time the width moves"). A LIVE
       `set-plugin-param` edit is not in that list — only `bypass_` and
       `savedState_` are — so the skew is set AGAIN after every width change
       here. Deliberate: the alternative is a case that silently measures a
       default-off instrument and passes its "channels are equal" half. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- ============================================================ PART A
         DirectGen: 0 -> C on a 2-channel page. One instance, channel for
         channel; the plugin writes STRAIGHT into the page channels. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.sine"
                   name="Smaragd Test Sine" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="0" nOut="2"
                   isInstrument="true"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="clap"
                            uid="tw.test.clap.sine" mode="DirectGen"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>

    <!-- One held C4 (key 60, 261.626 Hz) over 2 s: 120 BPM / ppq 960, so
         1920 ticks is a second and 3840 ticks is the whole clip. Every frame of
         both seconds is inside the note, so the bands below are closed forms
         rather than windows chosen to miss an edge. -->
    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="0" timePos="0" duration="3840" name="Hold"/>
    <add-note clip="0,0" tick="0" dur="3840" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>

    <!-- A1. THE DEFAULT IS OFF, and that is asserted rather than assumed —
         it is the whole reason no golden and no existing instrument_* case
         moves. Both channels carry the SAME closed form... -->
    <render filename="isr2_flat.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_flat.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_flat.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"/>
    <!-- ...and channel 1 is not merely at the same LEVEL, it is the same audio.
         (This is also the first assertion in the suite that says an
         INSTRUMENT's second output reaches the file at all: a DirectGen row
         that wrote only genOut_[0] into the page would leave channel 1 silent
         and fail the band above.) -->
    <assert-channels-differ filename="isr2_flat.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.0" minDiffRms="0.0001"
                            expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- A2. Skew ON. Channel 1 halves; channel 0 does not move. -->
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="0" slotIndex="0" paramId="3" value="1"/>

    <render filename="isr2_skew.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.27003" maxRms="0.28675"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.27003" maxRms="0.28675"/>

    <!-- The band must be able to FAIL, or the ratio proves nothing: channel 1
         is NOT at channel 0's level. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"
                         expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ...and it is the same SINE, an octave-flat copy of nothing: the pitch
         on channel 1 is the note's, so the halving is a gain and not a second
         voice. -->
    <assert-audio-frequency filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                            channel="1" minHz="260.626" maxHz="262.626"/>

    <!-- The level discriminator, in both of its independent forms: delta 0.2784
         and rms(ch0 - ch1) 0.2784 (the difference of a sine and half itself is
         half the sine). A sink that duplicated channel 0 gives 0 for both. -->
    <assert-channels-differ filename="isr2_skew.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>
    <assert-channels-differ filename="isr2_skew.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.20" minDiffRms="0.20"/>

    <!-- Nothing may sound after the note-off: the sine reports tailFrames() = 0
         honestly, on BOTH channels. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="100000" frameCount="18000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0001"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_skew.wav" startFrame="100000" frameCount="18000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0001"/>

    <!-- ============================================================ PART B
         GenFold: 0 -> 2 on a ONE-channel page. The pair is AVERAGED down,
         which the skew makes visible for the first time — with both outputs
         equal, "average the pair" and "take output 0" are the same number. -->
    <set-project-channels channels="1"/>
    <set-plugin-param trackPath="0" slotIndex="0" paramId="3" value="1"/>

    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="clap"
                            uid="tw.test.clap.sine" mode="GenFold"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>

    <render filename="isr1_fold.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <!-- 0.5 * (s + 0.5 s) = 0.75 s, so rms = 0.75 * 0.556769 = 0.417577. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr1_fold.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.40505" maxRms="0.43011"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr1_fold.wav" startFrame="48000" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.40505" maxRms="0.43011"/>
    <!-- ...and 0.556769 — "output 0 only", i.e. a fold that never happened —
         is 33 % away and outside it. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr1_fold.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.54007" maxRms="0.57349"
                         expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- The file really is ONE channel wide: channel 1 does not exist, which
         M0 made an ERROR rather than a silent rms 0. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr1_fold.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.0" maxRms="1.0"
                         expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ============================================================ PART C
         THE REFUSED ROW: 0 -> 2 on a SIX-channel page. 2 is neither 6 (Direct-
         Gen), nor 1 (MonoSpread), nor "2 on a mono page" (GenFold), nor wider
         than the page (WideGen) — so there is no defined spread and the slot
         loads TRANSPARENT and reports Unsupported. The instrument is silent:
         a track with a Transparent slot passes its own audio through, and this
         track has none. -->
    <set-project-channels channels="6"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="clap"
                            uid="tw.test.clap.sine" mode="Transparent"
                            state="Unsupported" hasFeed="1"/>

    <render filename="isr6_refused.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr6_refused.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0001"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr6_refused.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0001"/>
    <!-- Six channels wide, and no wider — the silence above is the mapping's
         refusal and not a one-channel file being read at channel 0. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr6_refused.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="5" minRms="0.0" maxRms="0.0001"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr6_refused.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="6" minRms="0.0" maxRms="1.0"
                         expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- ============================================================ PART D
         MonoSpread: 0 -> 1 on a 2-channel page. `tw.native.303` declares ONE
         audio output (twnativeinstrument.cc: "mono, like every other page in
         this engine"), so its single voice is COPIED to every page channel.

         THE TRAP THIS PART EXISTS FOR: the two channels being EQUAL is the
         RIGHT answer here, not a mono sink and not a bug. `assert-channels-
         differ` must therefore be REJECTED — and the per-channel energy bands
         beside it are what stop that rejection from also passing for silence.
         Read the two together or neither means anything. Centre-panned mono is
         all a pan-less clip model can express (design D3 / plugins inv. 37);
         when clips carry a pan this part is where it will show. -->
    <set-project-channels channels="2"/>
    <set-track-mute trackPath="0" muted="1"/>

    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <!-- No isInstrument attribute on purpose: `tw.native.303` is a BUILT-IN, so
         the registry resolves the flag. -->
    <insert-plugin trackPath="1" slotIndex="0"
                   format="tw" uid="tw.native.303"
                   name="Smaragd 303" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="" nIn="0" nOut="1"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="1" present="1" format="tw"
                            uid="tw.native.303" mode="MonoSpread"
                            state="Active" hasFeed="1"/>

    <insert-midi-clip trackPath="1" timePos="0" duration="3840" name="Hold"/>
    <add-note clip="1,0" tick="0" dur="3840" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>

    <render filename="isr2_spread.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="2.5"/>

    <!-- The 303 is a filtered saw with a decay envelope, so it gets the same
         qualitative band instrument_sine_render gives it: audible, not a closed
         form. On BOTH channels — a MonoSpread that copied only into channel 0
         would leave channel 1 silent and fail the second of these. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_spread.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="0" minRms="0.05" maxRms="1.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="isr2_spread.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                         channel="1" minRms="0.05" maxRms="1.0"/>
    <assert-audio-frequency filename="isr2_spread.wav" startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                            channel="1" minHz="259.626" maxHz="263.626"/>

    <!-- ...and they are the SAME audio, sample for sample. -->
    <assert-channels-differ filename="isr2_spread.wav"
                            channelA="0" channelB="1"
                            startFrame="0" frameCount="48000"
                            minRmsDelta="0.0" minDiffRms="0.0001"
                            expectReject="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
